/**
 * Switching Hysteresis Laboratory — four panels for the successor to
 * `rag-architecture-workload-uncertainty`. That topic estimated a workload that was assumed to
 * sit still. It does not sit still, and once it moves the question stops being *which*
 * architecture and becomes *when* to change.
 *   A. A workload that moves. The drifting path, and which arm each rule holds at every window.
 *      The best arm changes once; a rule that re-decides every window changes its mind a dozen
 *      times. Both trajectories are recomputed live.
 *   B. The control band. Switches and regret against the band width, averaged over every seed,
 *      recomputed live. Both ends of the range lose: the optimum is interior.
 *   C. Noise, not cost. The optimal band against the price of switching — already wide when
 *      switching is FREE, which is the topic's headline. Beside it the control: workloads with
 *      no drift at all, where every switch made is the estimator talking.
 *   D. Detecting is not deciding. The imported drift detectors against the policy on the same
 *      streams, failing in opposite directions.
 *
 * VIZ <-> PYTHON INVARIANT (CLAUDE.md): the block below is EMITTED from viz_constants() in
 * notebooks/rag-architecture-switching-hysteresis/rag_architecture_switching_hysteresis.py, and
 * test_laboratory_constants_match_the_module parses this file back and compares it to a fresh
 * bake. Only what needs SciPy is baked — the KS and PSI readings, and the static-workload table.
 * Everything else on this page, including every switch count, every regret and every optimal
 * band, is recomputed live in TypeScript from U_HAT_SEEDS and U_TRUE, which are the only two
 * arrays the policy is a function of. test_viz_constants_reproduce_the_policy checks that this
 * arithmetic lands on `simulate`, at the rounded precision the browser actually receives.
 */

